## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to extend a simple Azure Fundamentals stack with real feature-flag patterns, connecting front-end and back-end behavior to centralized configuration while maintaining security best practices.

## Key Concepts

- Azure App Configuration service and feature flags
- Front-end integration with App Configuration JavaScript SDK
- Server-side access via Azure AD managed identity
- GitHub Actions for infrastructure and configuration deployment
- Latency, caching, and cost optimization considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Front-end: fetch feature flag from App Configuration
const featureFlags = await fetchFeatureFlags();
if (featureFlags.enableWidget) {
  renderWidget();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical multi-tenant governance, cross-subscription access, and automation in a real-world analytics scenario. It tests data isolation, cost control, and operational readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Data Lake Gen2, Azure Synapse Spark
- Azure Lighthouse for cross-tenant access
- API Management as gateway
- Azure Policy, RBAC, tagging, encryption
- Terraform + GitHub Actions CI/CD, Cost Management budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: create Lighthouse assignment (illustrative)
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { LighthouseClient } = require('@azure/arm-lighthouse');
async function createAssignment() {
  const cred = new DefaultAzureCredential();
  const client = new LighthouseClient(cred, '<subscription-id>');
  await client.assignments.createOrUpdate('<scope>', '<assignment-name>', {
    properties: {
      principalId: '<principal-id>',
      roleDefinitionId: '/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions/...'
    }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-tenant access boundaries and revoke access when needed?
- What monitoring and alerting would you implement to catch misconfigurations early?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical cost optimization, data retention, and basic governance using Azure Storage lifecycle rules.

## Key Concepts

- Lifecycle Management rules
- Blob tiers and retention
- Soft delete and versioning
- Cost Management budgets and alerts

## Code Example

```json
{
  "rules": [
    {
      "name": "MoveToCool",
      "enabled": true,
      "type": "Lifecycle",
      "definition": {
        "filters": {
          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"],
          "prefixMatch": ["images/"]
        },
        "actions": {
          "baseBlob": {
            "tierToCool": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30},
            "delete": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 90}
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the lifecycle policy in a non-destructive way?
- What are trade-offs of Cool vs Archive tiers for image data?
